WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:01.901
-I loved seeing you.

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:02.968
Thanks so much for coming
on the show.

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.667
-I'm so happy
when I watch this show

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:05.834
and I'm happy when I'm here.

00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:07.267
That John Fogerty thing
was amazing.

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:08.834
-Wasn't that fun?
-It was amazing.

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:10.634
-He was amazing.
He crushed that one.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:13.000
-You playing the kazoo
like nobody's business.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.601
-Come on.
-I tried the jug and I go,

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:15.968
"I'm going to give up
on the jug."

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:17.167
Yeah, I couldn't really
play that.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:19.100
-But it was a mini kazoo solo,
so I liked it.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.300
-You heard that?
-I heard that.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.200
-Respect. See?
I love that you pay attention.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.834
You're always great. I know
that you're now living in L.A.,

00:00:24.834 --> 00:00:27.400
but I know you're from New York
and your show

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.534
was right here in the building.
-That's right.

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:32.234
-When you come back to New York,
does it feel like you're home?

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:35.067
-It feels like I'm home
and I run right to Broadway.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.667
-You do?
-Yeah, I run.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:39.868
-You always have
good recommendations.

00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:41.367
What did you see?
What did you see?

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:43.434
-First thing I saw
was Neil Diamond's show

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:45.100
"Beautiful Noise."
-I've not done it yet.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.200
-Now, you know, I thought
it was a jukebox musical.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.033
Just like "Mamma Mia."
A little story.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.167
No, I was sobbing at the end.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:55.701
It's the story of Neil Diamond
losing his ability

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:58.567
to play on tour
because he has Parkinson's.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.300
And his discussion
with a psychiatrist.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.267
And then Will Swenson.
You know him. He's unbelievable.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.334
Comes out, Tony Award winner,

00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:09.667
and he plays Neil Diamond
that we all know.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.467
And then at the end...
-No.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:14.501
-...they performed together
and mommy was sobbing.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:16.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:18.701
Jimmy, I was a blubbering mess.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.901
-"Oh, no, I'm going to lose it."
I love Neil Diamond.

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:23.133
-Oh, the finale. I don't want to
ruin it for anyone

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.501
but the finale --
Go see "Beautiful Noise."

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.400
Now, the next thing
you got to go see,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.601
everyone's trying
to get tickets.

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:30.167
"Sweeney Todd."
-I saw that one.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:33.033
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh, come on!

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.734
-The whole cast is amazing.
-Unbelievable.

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:36.934
-Josh Groban.
-Annaleigh.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:40.400
-Annaleigh Ashford. Oh, my gosh.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.434
-Annaleigh Ashford is
a Tony Award-winning

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:45.167
Lucille Ball
with a voice of a god.

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.267
Funny, beautiful, hysterical.

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.367
-How funny was she
in this thing?

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:51.100
Made me -- When she fell
down the stairs.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.234
-Fell down the stairs.

00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:55.167
-The funniest thing,
The funniest bit.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.634
I am like, "Wow." I was so --
-And you know that

00:01:57.634 --> 00:02:00.200
-Is not in the script.
You know, the old lady

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.534
falls down the stairs
on her heinie, cracking up

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:04.167
the audience was not
in the script.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:05.901
-She did that.
-She made that up.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.400
-She does it
and she does it every night.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.267
-Every night.
It was unbelievable.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.334
You have to go see.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.300
And the boy
from "Stranger Things."

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.901
-Gaten Matarazzo.
-Oh, my God was he so good.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:16.901
-Gaten Matarazzo was fantastic.

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:18.934
-Fantastic. What a voice.

00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:20.267
-And I've never seen
"Sweeney Todd."

00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:22.400
-I knew it.
I saw it with the original.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.634
So it was not funny, though,
in the original.

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:26.300
It's a depressing story.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.667
They're killing people
to make meat pies,

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:30.734
and then they're selling
the meat pies.

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:33.000
-It's a tough sell
if people don't know it.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.200
I go, "It's about a barber
who kills people

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.534
and uses the human meat
for meat pies."

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:39.934
And they go, "Okay."

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:42.534
But somehow -- And then you hear
this, like, "Pretty Women."

00:02:42.534 --> 00:02:44.300
-Oh, yeah.
-The songs you hear from there,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.667
you go,
♪ No one's going to harm you ♪

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.067
♪ Not while I'm around ♪

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.434
I go, "That's from
this crazy play?"

00:02:49.434 --> 00:02:52.167
-That's from this crazy play.
-It's beautiful.

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:54.434
-But it is so beautiful
and it's so funny.

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:55.901
And I loved every minute of it.

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:58.100
And I think there's going
to be Tonys all around.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.167
But the best thing that I saw

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:04.200
was Sean Hayes
in "Good night, Oscar."

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.300
-Really?
-Now, Jimmy...

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:09.567
...this was
an unbelievable performance.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.901
He plays a real guy,
Oscar Levant,

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:14.634
who had a lot of mental problems
and drug problems,

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:16.801
but was a brilliant wordsmith

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:19.601
and one of the intellectual
thinkers of the time.

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.467
He went on the Jack Paar show

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.701
in the middle
of having a nervous breakdown.

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:25.067
They took him
out of the hospital

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:27.234
for four hours on a leave,

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:30.334
had him perform on "The Tonight
Show" and then leave.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:31.968
-No.
-And it's a true story.

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:33.834
And Sean Hayes.
-I heard he's amazing.

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:36.067
-Oh, my God, Jimmy,
when he came back to say hello

00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:37.734
to all his guests,
I started crying.

00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:39.100
You know when you have that cry

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.200
that you didn't expect
to cry and you --

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.501
I just was like, "Oh, Sean,"
like, I couldn't stop crying.

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:46.968
-Isn't that a weird thing
about Broadway?

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.834
I feel so proud of them like
they're my kids or something.

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:51.601
-That's right.
-"I love you, buddy.

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:53.300
You did it. Get over here."

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.734
-Yes, "You do it eight times
a week. How do you do it?"

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.467
-I had nothing
to do with Sean Hayes at all.

00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:01.133
But yes, I'm hugging everybody
at the end like --

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:02.400
-He was -- Jimmy,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.734
it was an extraordinarily
brilliant performance

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:07.100
that I could not believe.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.501
I was witnessing a guy
that I've known and loved

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.501
for many years in this role.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:14.367
I was blown away.
He's going to win every award

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:15.734
and you should get tickets
if you can.

00:04:15.734 --> 00:04:17.434
-I love this.
-That's my publicity. Thank you.

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:18.968
Good night. Good night.
-That's all.

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:21.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.534
-But I love -- You're like --

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:24.734
Yeah, I go,
"I'll talk about your podcast."

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:26.133
You go, "Jimmy, I don't need to
talk about it."

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:27.634
-Yeah, whatever.
Yeah, I have a podcast

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:29.000
wherever podcasts are sold.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.534
"Rosie O'Donnell Onward."
-Yeah. There you go.

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:32.234
-Go. Okay.

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:35.067
-But you are no stranger
to Broadway not only supporting

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:37.300
but you actually --
I've seen you perform in --

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.634
Was it "Grease"?
-I was in "Grease."

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:41.501
You want to guess who I played?

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.934
-Yeah. Rizzo.
-Yeah. It wasn't Sandy.

00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:45.634
Sometimes people ask me --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.901
People go, "You were in
'Grease.' Who did you play?"

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:50.901
And I just wait a second.
And then I say, "Danny Zuko."

00:04:50.901 --> 00:04:52.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.267
-But you always say, you go,
"I can't really sing."

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:56.267
You can sing.

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:57.868
-Listen, I'm not a singer

00:04:57.868 --> 00:04:59.634
like the singers
you see on Broadway.

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:01.901
And I grew up loving
Barbra Streisand.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.267
And if that's the one
that you're going to hold high

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.300
as the highest voice you've ever
heard, you're nowhere near that.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.434
You know what I mean?
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.501
-So I know I can sing
funny patter songs

00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:12.934
and I know I can
do the comedy involved,

00:05:12.934 --> 00:05:14.901
but it's not like
they're putting on a new musical

00:05:14.901 --> 00:05:17.334
and they go, "You know,
can we get that Rosie O'Donnell?

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:20.033
Because that voice
is so soothing."

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.734
-People really -- No, yeah.

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:22.968
But you crushed it there.

00:05:22.968 --> 00:05:25.300
And you know what
you're also crushing is TikTok.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.501
Yes.
-TikTok loves you.

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:28.534
-I love TikTok.
You don't stop, right?

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:30.501
-TikTok. You don't stop.
-Exactly.

00:05:30.501 --> 00:05:32.033
-But how did you
get involved with TikTok?

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:34.200
-My daughter,
who's 20 years old

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.801
and was very bored
during the pandemic.

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:37.901
And her name is Vivi.

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:39.767
She's very beautiful,
very smart.

00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:43.400
And she told me
that I had to do one with her.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.767
And I'm like,
"I'm not going to dance.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:46.033
It's going to look ridiculous."

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:49.300
And so I did it just
because I'm a mom.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.701
-You'd do anything
for your kids.

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:52.801
-But you do anything to make
her happy for five minutes.

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:58.567
And then this kid
who was like a 20-year-old kid,

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:00.601
copied it and made fun of me.

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.200
And then I went on
and made fun of him.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.200
And then it became
like a thing, you know?

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.901
And I'm like, "You mean I can,
like, have fun

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.901
and play with strangers
on this thing?"

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:10.367
I think it's the best
invention in a long time.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:13.267
I hope they don't ban it because
I really enjoy my TikTok.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.534
-Yeah, no, I hope
they don't ban it either.

00:06:15.534 --> 00:06:17.267
-Yeah.
-You're good at it.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:19.601
-You can connect people to
people, you know,

00:06:19.601 --> 00:06:22.467
And I -- My daughter has autism
and she's 10 years old.

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:25.100
And I connect with
so many autism moms.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.901
And, you know,
when you have a niche like that

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:30.634
and you want to talk to someone
who really understands,

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:33.601
I've met people
and have real relationships

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:35.534
that serve my life
through doing that.

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.334
And sometimes
when you're famous,

00:06:37.334 --> 00:06:39.534
you don't get to meet
new people anymore.

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:42.434
You know, because people think
that you're different than them,

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:43.801
even though you're just
the same kid

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:45.000
that grew up on Long Island

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.434
and took the train
in to see "Pippin."

00:06:47.434 --> 00:06:49.200
You know, for the second act,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.501
and I would hide and
the ushers wouldn't find me,

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:55.100
and then I'd run and watch
Jill Clayburgh on stage

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.534
and John Rubenstein.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:06:58.534
-This way you can
connect to people and you go,

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:00.100
"Yeah, I was that person."

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.834
-Exactly.
And people, you know, on TikTok,

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:05.801
they don't seem so impressed
with your celebrity at all.

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:07.868
You know, they're kind of like,
"Oh, hi, Rosie,

00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:10.634
What are you doing on tonight?"
I'm like, "Yeah, frankly,

00:07:10.634 --> 00:07:13.200
I'm watching that
'Horrible Love is Blind.'"

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:18.334
The worst show on TV,
ever been on television.

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:20.200
-That's so funny.
-I'm going to hide in a booth

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.434
and only listen to you
for six weeks,

00:07:22.434 --> 00:07:25.367
and then I'm going to move in
with you for two months,

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:27.100
and then we're going
to see if it lasts.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.267
Good luck, kiddies.

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:29.601
-Sounds like something
I would watch. Yeah.

00:07:29.601 --> 00:07:31.801
-Yeah. I don't recommend.
-I think they can --

00:07:31.801 --> 00:07:35.334
Also, if you are successful
on the social media,

00:07:35.334 --> 00:07:36.934
it's because you're honest.

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:38.601
I think people
can smell a phony.

00:07:38.601 --> 00:07:40.033
-It's so true.
-They can sniff it out and they

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:41.501
go, "Oh, this is someone else.

00:07:41.501 --> 00:07:42.901
This is someone else
doing it for you.

00:07:42.901 --> 00:07:44.367
You're not being yourself.
You're not being real."

00:07:44.367 --> 00:07:46.601
-Right? And we've had
so much in our society

00:07:46.601 --> 00:07:49.667
and our culture with lies
and with misinformation

00:07:49.667 --> 00:07:52.634
and with fakeness
and what's real and what's not.

00:07:52.634 --> 00:07:55.133
And what do the Kardashians
look like before the surgeon?

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:56.367
[ Laughter ]

00:07:56.367 --> 00:07:58.968
You know?
Don't forget, honey.

00:07:58.968 --> 00:08:02.934
Don't forget. That's all tricky,
Pretend nothing, right?

00:08:02.934 --> 00:08:04.234
Don't strive for that.

00:08:04.234 --> 00:08:06.100
You know, you have to strive
for something real.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.434
And I think in our society
with art now,

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:12.601
with music, with our culture,
people want authenticity.

00:08:12.601 --> 00:08:15.200
They want to be told the truth.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.300
By you.
-Yeah. Real.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.033
-That's all that matters, right?

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:19.567
-That's why we love you.

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.434
I do want to talk
about your podcast,

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:23.634
"Onward with Rosie O'Donnell."

00:08:23.634 --> 00:08:25.000
It's basically
you were telling me

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.434
it's just you chatting
with friends.

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:28.667
-Pretty much. You know, I turned
60 and I went,

00:08:28.667 --> 00:08:30.300
"Listen, what do I want to do?"
-Happy birthday.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.367
-Thank you.
-If I'm lucky, I'm actually 61

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:35.634
now, but when I turned 60 --
-Happy belated 60th.

00:08:35.634 --> 00:08:37.767
-Thank you. When I turned 60, I
went, "You know what?

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:40.667
I'm going to rent myself a place
on the beach for one year.

00:08:40.667 --> 00:08:42.968
I'm going to get a chef
to cook me healthy food.

00:08:42.968 --> 00:08:44.067
I'm going to lose some weight.

00:08:44.067 --> 00:08:45.467
I'm going to feel better
about my life

00:08:45.467 --> 00:08:47.067
and I'm going to decide what do

00:08:47.067 --> 00:08:49.033
I want to do
with the last 30 summers

00:08:49.033 --> 00:08:51.400
should I be lucky enough
to live to 90.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.901
What do I want to do?"
Right?

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:56.133
[ Cheers and applause ]
And so that's why the podcast

00:08:56.133 --> 00:08:58.000
seemed like a wonderful thing,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.567
because I get to be home
with my kid and raise her

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:02.400
and I get to talk to friends

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.534
and still be a part
of the pop culture,

00:09:04.534 --> 00:09:07.100
but not so much in that it takes
over your world, you know?

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.200
-Good.
-That's what aging does.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.167
You don't give a crap anymore,
you know what I mean?

00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:13.334
Yeah.
[ Applause ]

00:09:13.334 --> 00:09:15.300
-I want to -- We're going
to go to commercial,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.033
but I just want to tell you that
when I started "Late Night,"

00:09:18.033 --> 00:09:20.167
I don't know if you remember
this, but you gave me

00:09:20.167 --> 00:09:21.734
such great advice.

00:09:21.734 --> 00:09:24.100
You were so nice to me.
You said

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.868
what's it like and what are you
doing for the audience

00:09:25.868 --> 00:09:28.667
and how are you going to warm
up, down to the last detail.

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.934
And I remember I was at
an event, like a charity event,

00:09:30.934 --> 00:09:32.434
and you were sitting next to me
and you go, "Jimmy,

00:09:32.434 --> 00:09:33.701
how's the 'Late Night'
thing going?

00:09:33.701 --> 00:09:35.267
You working hard?"
I go, "I am, Rosie."

00:09:35.267 --> 00:09:37.200
I'm writing, I'm working hard.
I really want this to win."

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.334
You go,
"Are you doing test shows?"

00:09:39.334 --> 00:09:41.901
I go, "I am."
And a test show doesn't air.

00:09:41.901 --> 00:09:44.000
And so you kind of ask
your friends or your neighbors

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.300
just "can you come
on a fake talk show

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.334
and have me interview?"
And I go, "I'm doing tests."

00:09:48.334 --> 00:09:49.834
And you go,
"I want to be on it."

00:09:49.834 --> 00:09:52.000
-Yeah.
-And you came on our test show

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.434
and I'll never, ever forget it.
-Yeah.

00:09:53.434 --> 00:09:55.367
-It was awesome.
-And you were great.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:09:58.000
And I'm so proud of you.
I really am.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.367
You're a wonderful man.

00:09:59.367 --> 00:10:00.968
-Rosie O'Donnell.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:00.968 --> 00:10:03.634
-Thank you.
-"Onward" wherever podcasts --

00:10:03.634 --> 00:10:06.767
-Anywhere. Anywhere.
-Anywhere!

00:10:06.767 --> 00:10:08.334
IHeart Radio app, wherever.

00:10:08.334 --> 00:10:10.334
We'll be right about with Suga,
everybody.

00:10:10.334 --> 00:10:11.801
Come on, stick around.

